<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Moira</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/637">637</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Mag Rath</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Moira, Mag Rath in Irish, was fought in 637 near Moira in County Down. The high king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-mac-aedo">Domnall mac Áedo</a> of the Northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congal-caech">Congal Cáech</a>, king of the Dál nAraidi, and his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-brecc">Domnall Brecc</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>; Congal was killed. The battle ended Ulaid hopes of the high kingship and weakened Dál Riata's hold on its lands in Ireland.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congal-caech">Congal Cáech</a> was recognised as King of Ulaid in 627. The next year Domnall, of the Uí Néill dynasty, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-king-of-ireland">High King of Ireland</a>, a position open to him because Congal had killed the previous High King, Suibne Menn, a distant cousin of Domnall's; some sources say Congal himself first claimed the high kingship after that victory. In 629 the two met at the Battle of Dún Ceithirn in what is now County Londonderry, and Congal was defeated and fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. In 637 he returned with the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>, whose army included many soldiers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Little is known of the fighting itself. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domnall-brecc">Domnall Brecc</a>'s army is said to have included Scots, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> and Britons, and about 50,000 men are reputed to have fought on each side. Congal probably landed near Dunseverick, while Domnall gathered his army at Tara and marched north to meet him. According to Sir Samuel Ferguson the battle may have lasted a week, ending with the defeated army in flight towards the woods of Killultagh. Domnall Brecc retreated north, and Congal was killed. When the Ulster Railway was built through Moira in the 19th century, the remains of thousands of men and horses were found.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On the same day the High King's fleet defeated that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> at the Battle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mull-of-kintyre">Mull of Kintyre</a>, and his forces then occupied the unprotected Dál Riata lands in north Antrim. Congal's death ended the chance for the Dál nAraidi and their allies to undo Domnall's advances, though the Ulaid were not completely subjugated. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> became dominant in the north of Ireland, and their descendants claimed overlordship of some of the land until the Flight of the Earls in 1607.</p><p>Townlands near Moira take their names from the battle: Aughnafosker, the Field of Slaughter, and Carnalbanagh, the Scotsman's Grave, where a stone pillar marking the graves of Scottish princes stood until a farmer removed it in the late 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Moira” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Domnall Brecc joined Congal Cáech of the Ulaid in his war against the Irish high king and was defeated at the <b>Battle of Mag Rath</b> in 637, which weakened Dál Riata’s position in Ireland; he was killed fighting the Britons of Strathclyde at Strathcarron in 642.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat at <b>Mag Rath</b> in 637 under Domnall Brecc, and later Pictish conquest, eroded its standing.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-ngabrain">Cenel nGabrain</a></li><li><blockquote>Congal Cáech, king of the Ulaid and briefly claimant to the kingship of Tara, allied with Dál Riata but was defeated and killed by Domnall mac Áedo at <b>Mag Rath</b> in 637.</blockquote><a href="/state/dal-naraidi">Dal nAraidi</a></li><li><blockquote>Kings such as Báetán mac Cairill and Congal Cáech challenged the Uí Néill, but defeat at <b>Mag Rath</b> in 637 ended their hopes of wider supremacy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ulaid">Kingdom of Ulaid</a></li></ul></section>
